  "query_preview": "..Today and Thursday...A trough is visible in \nsatellite imagery, currently centered over northern Nevada. This \nfeature is forecast to follow an eastward trajectory through Utah \nand into Colorado by this evening. Most precipitation with this \ntrough will stay well north of Arizona, with just a slight (10%) \nchance of light showers near the Four Corners. The primary impact \nwill be some breezy west to northwest winds with gusts as high as \n25-35 mph for the higher terrain along/north of the Mogollon Rim \nand White Mountains. Other than that, expect daytime temperatures \na few degrees below normal. Lighter winds and continued dry \nconditions are expected on Thursday following the departure of the\ntrough. \n\nFriday through Monday...Another weak shortwave approaches on Friday \nand may bring just enough moisture and lift for some showers over \nthe higher terrain of eastern Arizona. Into the weekend, ensemble \nguidance generally favors a setup with a weak cut-off low off the \ncoast of Baja California and high pressure over the Great Basin. \nExpect increasing heights aloft and warming temperatures, approaching\nnear climatological normals this weekend. We will also see surface\npressure gradients shift to support northeast winds. Some highly \nlocalized northeast gusts up to 20-30 mph will be possible \nalong/south of the Mogollon Rim, especially on Saturday morning \nand Sunday morning. \n\nTuesday and onward...Long term ensemble guidance hints at the \npotential for some weak shortwaves to propagate overhead early\nnext week and a very slight chance for showers on Tuesday. A \ndeeper pacific trough may try to move across our region by the \nmiddle- late part of next week, but model agreement breaks down at\nthis point and this solution is not a guarantee.",
